2)Nodeport >> Will assign a port to the node and 

A NodePort service opens a gateway for external access to your cluster and web application. It operates by exposing a specific port at the node level within Kubernetes. Traffic hitting this node port is then forwarded to the corresponding service, which in turn routes it to your web application.


apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: webserver-nodeport
  namespace: web
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: webserver
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 80
      nodePort: 30007

port: 80	Service internal port (ClusterIP port)
targetPort: 80	Container port on Pod
nodePort: 30007	External port opened on Node



3) LoadBalancer service:

Now that we’ve seen the limitations of ClusterIP for internal-only communication and NodePort’s shortcomings for production use due to its lack of traffic balancing, let’s explore the LoadBalancer service. As the name suggests, a LoadBalancer service in Kubernetes facilitates external access to your service through a cloud provider’s load balancer. This setup allows external traffic to be distributed efficiently to your service. Essentially, creating a LoadBalancer service in Kubernetes triggers the automatic provisioning of an external load balancer by your cloud provider, seamlessly integrating cloud infrastructure to manage traffic flow.


apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: webserver-loadbalancer
  namespace: web
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: webserver
  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 80

Applying this YAML doesn’t just create a service; it also signals your cloud provider to set up a load balancer. Initially, the EXTERNAL-IP field might show as 'pending' while the load balancer is being provisioned.



4) ExternalName- A Kubernetes ExternalName Service is a special type of service that maps a service name within the cluster to an external DNS name. 

externalname.yml

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-database
spec:
  type: ExternalName
  externalName: db-prod.example.com



